clean_summary:
Nice open-source release from Poolside! I really like the architecture. Some unusual hidden modeling details include the use of per-head gated attention with softplus instead of sigmoid, applied to both SWA and full attention, which is why they don't use sink. There's a non-standard 'moe_router_logit_softcapping' parameter set to 0 by default, indicating they likely encountered instabilities in the router scores, which also affects the bias strength in the top-k.

Additionally, they have more attention heads on SWA layers, similar to Xiaomi V2 Flash, which used higher KV heads for SWA layers. They also use a different beta_fast for Yarn than most models (64 instead of 32) and apply partial (half) rope for the full attention layers.

@poolsideai: "Today we’re releasing Laguna XS.2, Poolside’s first open-weight model. It’s a 33B total / 3B active MoE model built for agentic coding and long-horizon tasks. Trained fully in-house on our own stack. Runs on a single GPU. Released under Apache 2.0."

It's hard to know if this is real since the efficiency numbers look really insane (52x faster than Flash Attention at 1M), but the evaluation numbers don't seem unbelievable if we consider it's a continual pretraining of one of the recent open-source models.

One red flag is that they did a paid partnership on X, but that might just be their way to do marketing. It seems like there's a tech report coming soon; it would be huge if it's legit.

@alex_whedon: "Introducing SubQ - a major breakthrough in LLM intelligence. It is the first model built on a fully sub-quadratic sparse-attention architecture (SSA), and the first frontier model with a 12 million token context window which is 52x faster than FlashAttention at 1MM tokens and less than 5% the cost of Opus. Transformer-based LLMs waste compute by processing every possible relationship between words (standard attention). Only a small fraction actually matter. @subquadratic finds and focuses only on the ones that do. That's nearly 1,000x less compute and a new way for LLMs to scale."

Well played, I guess.

@xai: "SpaceXAI will provide @AnthropicAI with access to Colossus 1, one of the world’s largest and fastest-deployed AI supercomputers, to provide additional capacity for Claude."

The partnership aims to enhance the computational resources available for Claude by leveraging Colossus 1's capabilities.

The SpaceX and Anthropic compute deal went under the radar, but the features make a lot of sense for power users and are expected to become very important soon. There is a vision for better and more expressive memory with cross-session context to detect patterns.

This could lead to a more advanced version of Codex 'goals', with a separate grader to control quality, safety, and reward hacking for long-horizon tasks in the coming months. Additionally, there is a focus on orchestrating agents, which serves to preserve the context window of the 'main agent', an important aspect for functionality.

@claudeai: "Dreaming reviews your agent's past sessions, extracts patterns, and curates memories so your agents learn over time."

This is nice, but there is no comparison on speed or acceptance rate versus classical MTP (for instance, Stepfun Flash 3.5 is 1 SWA layer + MLP block) or Eagle, which would have been useful to see if this is an improvement. There are also a few inconsistencies: the Google blog says up to 3x speed, the Hugging Face readme says 2x, and the generation file mentions num_assistant_tokens=6, suggesting they had more MTP layers initially.

On the architecture itself, a few additional details include that the cluster head thing is only in the smaller model, while the larger variant does KV sharing across all layers + K=V (like the main model), which is very aggressive. They further reduce the number of attention heads in the small model.

@iamgrigorev: "Very nice MTP modification for Gemma4. It uses 4 decoder layers with 3 SWA + 1 GA. After embedding + hidden concatenation, it downprojects to 256 (instead of hidden size), which makes computation much faster. Before the LM head, it upprojects back to hidden size. It has a much more efficient LM head in the MTP by selecting clusters of logits! Definitely worth looking into."

Cursor is at frontier scale, both in terms of performance and compute. If Composer 2.5's budget were put into a pre-train, it would result in approximately 6.3 trillion total parameters, with 200 billion actively trained on around 56 trillion tokens. If Composer 3 allocates 50% of the budget to pre-training, it would yield about 500 billion active parameters and 15.3 trillion total trained on 135 trillion tokens.

These assumptions serve as a lower bound, considering factors like 35% Most Frequently Used (MFU), FP8, and around 3-4% sparsity similar to K2, along with H100 efficiency. The model/token allocation is based on the mean between K2 and K2.5 data points and Inclusion AI's compute optimal rules for Mixture of Experts (MoE). The progression between Composer 2 and Composer 2.5 is impressive.

@cursor_ai: "Introducing Composer 2.5, our most powerful model yet. It's more intelligent, better at sustained work on long-running tasks, and more reliable at following complex instructions. For the next week, we’re doubling the included usage of the model."

Glad to see this — renderers are a foundational component of the LLM stack. They map between tokens and messages, which are invariant to tokenizer and formatting details. Most APIs, datasets, and RL environments are defined in terms of messages.

Getting the details wrong leads to train-test mismatches, caching inefficiencies, and prompt injection vulnerabilities. A renderers module was included in Tinker Cookbook, but it makes sense as a standalone library.

@PrimeIntellect: "Introducing Renderers. RL trainers work in tokens. Environments work in messages. Going back and forth corrupts sampled tokens, wasting compute on every agentic turn. With Renderers, we fix this mismatch. This unlocks more than 3x throughput on popular open models."

Scaling laws are a beautiful part 2. This is a very clean approach to pretraining ablation, similar to MAI scaling ladder and fully open.

@classiclarryd: "Building momentum at Marin! Upgrading from Dense to 129B parameter MoEs, architecture improvements, and optimizer improvements gives our pretraining recipe an estimated 6x cumulative learning speedup, accounting for MFU. Includes community contributions."

Open Athena is a nonprofit that accelerates academia with capabilities from the AI frontier.

NVIDIA 💚🦋 Prime Intellect. 

@PrimeIntellect: "We're excited to join the NVIDIA Nemotron Coalition. Frontier open models matter for the whole ecosystem. We're bringing the RL infrastructure and environments we've built over the last year to help scale agentic capabilities."

Prime Intellect joins the NVIDIA Nemotron Coalition alongside leading open model builders, contributing post-training infrastructure, over 2,500 RL environments, and a hosted loop to help specialize Nemotron for real-world applications.
